course: dictionary
lesson: 9962
title: Taxation: money for the things we share
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Taxation is the system of taking money, called taxes, from people and businesses. The government uses that money to pay for things everyone shares, like schools, roads, hospitals and police. When the word is used, it can mean the whole system or the amount of money taken.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «The colonists were angry that Britain taxed them though they had no say in Parliament» shows taxed is the verb, and taxation is the noun for the same idea. The people were angry because money was taken from them without them having a say.
explain: Taxation can be high or low. High taxation means the government takes a large share of money from people and businesses. Low taxation means it takes a smaller share. Historians use the phrase unfair taxation when they explain why people rebelled.
fact: Taxation is the system of taking money, called taxes, from people and businesses.
fact: Governments use taxes to pay for schools, roads, hospitals and police.
fact: «No taxation without representation» was a cry used by the colonists, who felt they should have a say before being taxed.
fact: In the Boston Tea Party of 1773, protesters threw tea into the harbour to protest against taxation.
fact: In maths, a tax is often a percent of a price or income, so you can work it out with percentages.
exercise: What is taxation? | *The system of taking money from people and businesses to pay for things a government provides | A fine for breaking a rule | A map of a city's roads | A type of tea sold in shops
exercise: Which of these does taxation help to pay for? | Sweets and toys | Birthday parties | *Schools and roads | Holidays for the whole town
exercise: A shop adds a tax of 10 percent to a £20 price. How much is the tax? | *£2 | £20 | £0.20 | £10
test: Why were the colonists angry about taxation? | *They were taxed though they had no say in Parliament | They had no roads to travel on | They had too many schools | They were given free tea
test: Which phrase means the government takes a large share of money? | Barely taxation | Ancient taxation | Glum taxation | *High taxation
exam: What does taxation mean? | A rule that bans roads | *The money taken from people and businesses to pay for things a government provides | A school for young children | A cup of tea with milk
